@meta
id: 14-07
title: Between two marks
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-range
par: 64
minutes: 6
vscode: select the staging block, Ctrl-H in selection
open: environments.ts

@brief
Staging has moved into its own DNS zone. Every host in the `STAGING`
block that ends in `.internal` now ends in `.staging.internal`. The
`PRODUCTION` block underneath is line for line identical and must not
change.

The block is in the middle of the file, so neither `%` nor `.,$` fits,
and you are not going to count lines. Mark where the block starts,
mark where it ends, and give the substitute the two marks as its range.
Chapter 9 used a mark as the target of a delete; here it is the edge of
a range.

The dot in `.internal` is a regex character. Say what you mean.

@keys
| `ma` | set mark `a` on this line |
| `:'a,'bs/old/new/` `Enter` | substitute from the line of mark `a` to the line of mark `b` |
| `}` | jump to the next blank line — the end of the block |
| `\.` | a literal dot in a pattern |

@hints
Search to the `STAGING` line, drop a mark there. Get to the end of the
block and drop another. Then the range is the two marks.
--
`ma` sets mark `a`. In a range, a mark is written `'a`, so the range
from mark `a` to mark `b` is `'a,'b`. The blank line after the block
is a fine place for `b`; there is nothing on it to change.
--
`/STAGING` `Enter`, `ma`, `}`, `mb`, then
`:'a,'bs/\.internal/.staging.internal/` and Enter. The `g` is not
needed; each line has one host.

@solution
    /STAGING<Enter>  ma  }  mb  :'a,'bs/\.internal/.staging.internal/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-six keystrokes, and the two marks were set by motions you
already own, not by counting.

A mark as a range address is written with a leading apostrophe: `'a`
is the line mark `a` is on. `'a,'b` is therefore the same shape as
`8,19`, with the numbers looked up for you. Everything about the range
stays true if lines are added above the block, which is the weakness
`8,19` had. Vim keeps a few marks of its own that work the same way:
`'<` and `'>` are the start and end of the last visual selection, and
chapter 22 will show you that selecting a block and pressing `:` fills
in `'<,'>` automatically. Until then, `a` and `b` are your hands.

The escaped dot is not decoration. `.internal` as a pattern means "any
character, then internal", which happens to match the same five lines
here and would happily match `xinternal` or `-internal` in a file
where those existed. Chapter 13 taught you the difference; this is
where you keep honouring it even when it would not have bitten you.

@start environments.ts
// Connection strings per environment. Secrets are injected at boot.

export const STAGING = {
  database: 'mysql://database.internal:3306/orders',
  rabbit: 'amqp://rabbit.internal:5672/payments',
  cache: 'redis://cache.internal:6379',
  gateway: 'https://gateway.internal/graphql',
  email: 'smtp://mail.internal:587',
};

export const PRODUCTION = {
  database: 'mysql://database.internal:3306/orders',
  rabbit: 'amqp://rabbit.internal:5672/payments',
  cache: 'redis://cache.internal:6379',
  gateway: 'https://gateway.internal/graphql',
  email: 'smtp://mail.internal:587',
};

@expected environments.ts
// Connection strings per environment. Secrets are injected at boot.

export const STAGING = {
  database: 'mysql://database.staging.internal:3306/orders',
  rabbit: 'amqp://rabbit.staging.internal:5672/payments',
  cache: 'redis://cache.staging.internal:6379',
  gateway: 'https://gateway.staging.internal/graphql',
  email: 'smtp://mail.staging.internal:587',
};

export const PRODUCTION = {
  database: 'mysql://database.internal:3306/orders',
  rabbit: 'amqp://rabbit.internal:5672/payments',
  cache: 'redis://cache.internal:6379',
  gateway: 'https://gateway.internal/graphql',
  email: 'smtp://mail.internal:587',
};
